## 2. Core Technical Stack

Use the following stack:

- Next.js with the App Router.
- React.
- TypeScript with strict type checking.
- Tailwind CSS.
- shadcn/ui where appropriate.
- Lucide React for icons.
- Framer Motion or Motion for subtle animations.
- Local JSON files as the source of truth for website content.
- Server Components for content-driven pages wherever possible.
- Client Components only for interactive features such as search, filtering, mobile navigation, and clipboard actions.
- A suitable Markdown renderer for educational content.
- Zod for validating JSON content and configuration.
- Next.js Metadata API for SEO.
- Vercel for deployment.

Use current, stable, mutually compatible package versions and follow the official Next.js App Router conventions.

Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.

### Frontend-only architecture

The website must not require:

- A backend server.
- A database.
- An external CMS.
- Authentication.
- An admin dashboard.
- Server actions that mutate persistent data.
- API routes for ordinary content retrieval.
- A paid third-party service to display content.

All published content must be bundled with the application from repository files.

Browser-side interactions may use standard browser APIs for clipboard access, local search, filtering, and other interface features.

The website must deploy on Vercel using the standard Next.js deployment workflow.

Prefer a statically generated website wherever practical. Use `generateStaticParams` for content-driven routes and avoid request-time server dependencies.

If a feature requires persistent storage, explain the limitation and provide a suitable frontend-only alternative. Do not introduce a database simply to support optional functionality.

