## 9. Learning Hub

Create an educational resource platform at `/learn`.

The learning system must use a flexible hierarchy.

```text
Course
  └── Module
       └── Lesson
            ├── Written Notes
            ├── Code Examples
            ├── PDFs
            ├── Presentations
            ├── Exercises
            ├── Worksheets
            ├── External Links
            └── Other Learning Materials
```

A course can have any number of modules.

A module can have any number of lessons.

A lesson can have any number of resources of different types.

Do not assume all courses contain the same number of modules or lessons.

### A. Course listing

At `/learn`, show all published courses.

Support:

- Search by course title and description.
- Subject filters.
- Topic filters.
- Difficulty filters.
- Featured courses.
- Course cards.
- Optional pagination or load-more functionality.

Course cards should display title, description, subject, level, and the number of published modules or lessons.

Calculate counts from actual published content rather than hardcoding them.

### B. Course detail

At `/learn/[courseSlug]`, display:

- Course title.
- Description.
- Subject and level.
- Learning objectives.
- Prerequisites.
- Course duration if provided.
- Module list.
- Lesson counts.
- Published learning materials.
- Related courses.
- Start learning or continue browsing CTA.

Only show published modules and lessons.

### C. Module detail

At `/learn/[courseSlug]/[moduleSlug]`, display:

- Course breadcrumb.
- Module title and description.
- Module learning objectives.
- Ordered lesson list.
- Lesson summaries.
- Lesson publication status where appropriate.
- Previous and next module navigation when applicable.

### D. Lesson detail

At `/learn/[courseSlug]/[moduleSlug]/[lessonSlug]`, create a comfortable reading experience.

Include:

- Breadcrumb navigation.
- Course and module information.
- Lesson title.
- Reading time if available.
- Table of contents generated from headings.
- Formatted written content.
- Code blocks with syntax highlighting.
- Copy-code buttons.
- Downloadable materials.
- External resource links.
- Related lessons.
- Previous and next lesson navigation.
- Sidebar or collapsible course outline.
- Mobile-friendly lesson navigation.

Support Markdown or MDX-style educational content where appropriate.

Use a consistent, safe content-rendering strategy. Do not execute arbitrary code embedded in lesson content.

Use the appropriate syntax highlighting library if needed, and ensure code examples remain readable on small screens.

### E. Learning progress

Optionally provide a lightweight frontend-only way to mark lessons as completed using localStorage.

If implemented:

- Explain through the interface that progress is stored in the current browser.
- Do not require an account.
- Do not claim progress synchronizes across devices.
